= Genetic inheritance (technical) =

== Processes and transmission channels ==
Distinguish (a) replication fidelity and mutation, (b) segregation of replicated chromosomes, (c) recombination and reassortment, (d) vertical descent, and (e) horizontal genetic exchange. In a simple diploid autosomal Mendelian model, heterozygote Aa produces A and a gametes in equal proportions absent segregation distortion, but linkage, selection, polyploidy and other mechanisms qualify generalization.

== Bacterial dynamics ==
Horizontal gene transfer can be mediated through transformation, conjugation, phage-associated transduction and mobile elements. Gene acquisition is not identical to stable inheritance: maintenance, fitness cost, regulatory compatibility and selection determine persistence. Bacterial phylogenies may be non-tree-like at particular loci, complicating inference from sequence similarities alone.

== Epigenetic distinction ==
A transmitted DNA sequence change is different from inheritance of an altered regulatory state. Transgenerational epigenetic claims require clear generation accounting and evidence excluding direct exposure; the role of methylation, histone marks and RNA varies among organisms. See Fitz-James and Cavalli (2022).

== Computational framing ==
Information-theoretic measures over genotype distributions may quantify variation, but Shannon bits alone do not encode biological function. Models of inheritance require explicit assumptions about population structure, mutation, recombination, selection and sampling; a shared sequence is not proof of an unambiguous causal lineage when horizontal transfer or convergence is possible.

